00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,480
Go to Tencent AI Studio

118
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,480
If the website appears in Chinese

119
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:39,480
Simply right click and translate the page to English

120
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:43,480
Create an account and open Multi-Modal Experience

121
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:47,480
Then select HY Video 1.5 I2V

122
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:51,480
This model is completely free and open source

123
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:55,480
Upload your character image and paste the animation prompt shown on screen

124
00:06:55,480 --> 00:07:00,480
Full Body Character Walk Cycle Fixed Side Profile View

125
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,480
Walking to the Right

126
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:05,480
After about 2 minutes you'll get a cute animated walk cycle

127
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:09,480
If you don't like the result just regenerate or change the prompt

128
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:12,480
Feel free to experiment with different animations

129
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:16,480
When I prototype games I usually create two animations first

130
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,480
Walk and Idle

131
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:21,480
That's already enough to start testing gameplay

132
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:24,480
Part 11

133
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:27,480
Exporting transparent frames in Blender

134
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:32,480
Now we'll turn the animation into transparent PNG frames for the game

135
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:36,480
Open Blender and choose Video Editing

136
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:38,480
Click Add Movie

137
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:41,480
Then import your animation

138
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:44,480
Next, choose the frame range you want for the loop

139
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:49,480
Go to Output, Frame Range and change the start and end frames

140
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:53,480
You want the first and last frames to look similar

141
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:55,480
So the animation loops smoothly

142
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:11,480
Press the space bar to preview the animation

143
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:14,480
Now we'll remove the green screen

144
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:18,480
Inside the strip modifier settings, add modifier

145
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,480
Compositor

146
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:22,480
New

147
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:28,480
Open the Compositor workspace

148
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:31,480
And enable Sequencer Compositor nodes

149
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:39,480
Then press Add

150
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:40,480
Keying

151
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:41,480
Keying

152
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:44,480
And place the node between the existing nodes

153
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:46,480
Click on Key Color

154
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:49,480
Use the dropper tool and sample the green background

155
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:52,480
Now your video has transparency

156
00:08:57,480 --> 00:09:01,480
To export the frames, go to Output properties

157
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:03,480
And adjust the resolution if needed

158
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:09,480
Choose where you want to save your files

159
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:12,480
Under Media Type, choose Image

160
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:14,480
File Format, PNG

161
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:15,480
Color

162
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:17,480
RGBA

163
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:19,480
Then go to Render

164
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:20,480
Render Animation

165
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:24,480
Blender will export all frames as transparent PNG images

166
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:26,480
Ready for your game

167
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:28,480
And one final tip

168
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:30,480
Save your Blender project file

169
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:34,480
That way you can reuse the exact same setup for future animations

170
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:47,480
And that's it

